// QW — how someone joins the network.
// Source of truth: protocol/nips/NIP-QW07-introduction.md (kind 9060),
// NIP-QW06 (9050/9051), NIP-QW03 (skill tags), protocol/src/identity.rs.
// There is no registration endpoint anywhere in this picture — that is the
// point of it.

title Joining QW

Newcomer [icon: user, color: violet]
Hop1 [icon: user-check, label: "Contact (hop 1)"]
Network [icon: share-2, label: "Contact graph"]

Newcomer > Newcomer: Identity::generate()\nsecp256k1 -> did:key + Nostr pubkey

opt [label: "found someone first — NIP-QW06"] {
  Newcomer > Hop1: private ask (out of propagation scope)
  Hop1 > Network: skill query 9050, signed by Hop1 about itself
  Network > Hop1: skill answer 9051
  Hop1 > Newcomer: candidate contacts
}

alt [label: "self-introduction"] {
  Newcomer > Hop1: 9060 subject_pubkey == event.pubkey\nchain: []
}
else [label: "mutual introduction"] {
  Hop1 > Network: 9060 subject_pubkey = Newcomer\nchain: connecting hops, oldest first
}

Newcomer > Network: 9020 profile skill tags\nevidence = completed contracts

// Both shapes are signed, so the introducer's reputation is attached to the
// claim. That is the whole admission control: an edge exists because someone
// staked their name on it, not because a form was submitted.
Network > Newcomer: edge exists — you are in the graph
